<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jun 30, 2022">
<CPscript title="The 'Cookie' method" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>This is an introductory script to give the user an early taste of what CirclePack can do. Run through the script with "NEXT"; later you can come back to look at it in greater depth.</description>
<text> A (discrete) conformal mapping is a mapping f: Q --> P between  two univalent circle packings sharing the same combinatorics K. (A circle packing is 'univalent' if its circles have mutually disjoint interiors.) Thus, f maps every circle of Q (or every face, if you prefer) to the corresponding circle (or face) of P.

We open here with a target plane domain Omega on the right. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" tooltip="Start over" iconname="dnd_multi.png">act 1;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read overlay.p;infile_path kurve.g;disp -g; </cmd>
<text> The "cookie" method is one of two we will discuss for building discrete conformal mappings from the disc to such target regions. It first creates a packing P in a target region Omega by cookie-cutting it from a larger euclidean packing. (This is the construction suggested by William Thurston in 1985 when he got this topic started.) You are welcome, at this point, to read in a different Jordan curve if you wish (see 'infile_path').

In the next two inline commands we first overlay Omega with a hexagonal circle packing of the whole plane, then we cookie-cut the image packing P. There are intervening optional commands 'i' and 'd' for, respectively, increasing or decreasing the radii of this hexagonal packing before you cookie. (The cookie method will be seen again in 'DiscreteRMT.xmd'.) </text>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">act 1;Disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="i" mnemonic="i" tooltip="increase all radii by 20%" iconname="button_ok.png">adjust_rad -p1 1.2 a;layout -p1;Disp -p1 -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" tooltip="decrease all radii by 20%" iconname="centrejust.png">adjust_rad -p1 .9 a;layout -p1;Disp -p1 -w -c -g; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="centrejust.png">act 1;cookie;Disp -w -c -g; </cmd>
<text> Note that along with P we get its complex K, embedded here as the carrier of P. </text>
<cmd iconname="dnd_multi.png">act 1;disp -w -f -g </cmd>
<text> Sending a copy of the packing in p1 to p0, we create the domain by converting p0 to hyperbolic geometry, setting the boundary radii to infinite hyperbolic radius, repacking, and laying out. That is, we compute the maximal packing Q in p0. </text>
<cmd iconname="psi.png">copy -p1 0;act 0;geom_to_h;set_rad -.1 b;repack;layout; Disp -w -c; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">color -c s b;put_data -q1 -cc b;Disp -p0 -w -c -cf b;Disp -p1 -w -c -cf b -g; </cmd>
<text> Here we see the maximal packing Q as domain on the left, and the image packing P on the right. The mapping is f: Q --> P is a discrete conformal mapping from the unit disc to Omega. The boundary circles have been color coded to help with identifications. The user can also click on circles to see the correspondence. </text>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
    <AboutImage name="AboutImage.jpg">

    </AboutImage>
